      "question": {
        ":question/id": "03a41afd-2d26-5621-82d5-f6aaa5db641b",
        ":question/problem": "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)x=6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-3(q+4),\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(q+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-3(q+4)}{-3} & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\ q+4 & =0 \\\\ q & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
